preConditionHook:
// BROOKS'S TRAP TEST ON A SECOND ENTRY: "if the second entry is letting you in
// at a better price than the first, be suspicious. Most good second entries are
// at the same price or worse." A second buy signal whose entry price is BELOW
// the first one's is the market still going down, not a pullback ending. Figure
// 10.1's bar 10 is his counter-example -- one tick better than bar 9, and it
// failed.
//
// THE SCAN IS SecondSignal's, INLINED -- rules in this corpus never reference
// each other -- and carries its comment's substance unchanged: the signal is the
// GENERIC price trigger, a close above the prior bar's high, and the failure is
// price trading back beyond that signal bar's opposite extreme. Bar kk is the
// earlier trigger bar and bar kk + 1 its SIGNAL bar, whose high is the first
// entry's price; the failure window is the kk - 1 bars ENDING AT THE CURRENT
// BAR, which is why the outer scan starts at 2 -- at kk = 2 that window is the
// current bar alone and the inner loop does not execute, in EasyLanguage and
// here alike. THE LOSS IS REAL AND DELIBERATE: this counts a second GENERIC
// trigger, not a second instance of whichever pattern the pane is trading.
//
// THE PRICE COMPARED IS THIS BAR'S OWN HIGH, per the merged pseudocode and the
// corpus's signal-bar-close / next-bar-open convention: the bar that fires IS
// the signal bar, so its high is where a buy stop would go, and the first
// entry's price is high[kk + 1]. A long passes when this bar's entry price is
// at or ABOVE the first one's, less toleranceTicks of slack; the short mirrors
// it at the low.
//
// WHICH FIRST SIGNAL. The scan can find several; the one kept is the MOST
// RECENT, the smallest kk, because kk ascends and the retained price is written
// only while the flag is still false. The FLAG's meaning is unchanged from
// SecondSignal -- true if ANY kk qualifies -- so the two rules agree bar for bar
// on that half.
//
// THIS IS A FILTER, so it carries the scan's finding and the price gate but NOT
// SecondSignal's own bar-0 trigger (close[0] > high[1]): the placement ANDs it
// with whatever entry it is gating, and SecondSignal supplies the trigger when
// it is the one being gated. At a very large toleranceTicks the rule degenerates
// to SecondSignal without its trigger, which is the intended superset relation
// and the reason Brooks states it as an override rather than a separate setup.
//
// Both sides are computed in the hook: EasyLanguage does not short-circuit, so a
// scan inside a condition would run on every bar in EL and only on some bars in
// C++.
// THE TICK is EL's own MinMove / PriceScale, both VERIFIED and per-symbol
// constants for the whole run, written DIVISION-FIRST in both twins so the C++
// reads token for token against its EasyLanguage. Read once into a local.
//
// Scratch below is declared in localVariables, not at hook scope: the generator
// only prefixes declared locals, so a hook-scope declaration is a redefinition
// when this rule is placed twice in one strategy. Every one is fully assigned
// each bar before it is read.
tickSz = ctx.MinMove() / ctx.PriceScale();
secondUp = false;
secondDn = false;
firstHigh = 0.0;
firstLow = 0.0;
for (kk = 2; kk <= (int)withinBars; ++kk) {
    runLow = low[0];
    runHigh = high[0];
    for (jj = 1; jj <= kk - 2; ++jj) {
        if (el_lt(low[jj], runLow)) runLow = low[jj];
        if (el_gt(high[jj], runHigh)) runHigh = high[jj];
    }
    if (el_gt(close[kk], high[kk + 1]) && el_lt(runLow, low[kk + 1]) && !secondUp) {
        secondUp = true;
        firstHigh = high[kk + 1];
    }
    if (el_lt(close[kk], low[kk + 1]) && el_gt(runHigh, high[kk + 1]) && !secondDn) {
        secondDn = true;
        firstLow = low[kk + 1];
    }
}
